    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_pyf_421",
      "name": "dataclass-and-type-hint-behaviour-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "What does a dataclass really do?",
      "description": "Predict what generated dataclass methods and type hints do at runtime before you rely on them in a script.",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Sku:\n    code: str\n    qty: int = 0\n\na = Sku(\"MUG\", 2)\nb = Sku(\"MUG\", 2)\n\nWhich statements are true? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "@dataclass generates __eq__, which compares the fields in order, so a == b is True even though a and b are two different objects (a is b is False). frozen=True makes the generated __setattr__ raise dataclasses.FrozenInstanceError: cannot assign to field 'qty'. A dataclass with eq=True (the default) and frozen=True also gets a __hash__ built from its fields, so a and b have the same hash and the set {a, b} holds one element. A mutable default such as tags: list[str] = [] is rejected when the class is defined: ValueError: mutable default <class 'list'> for field tags is not allowed: use default_factory. The instances never get the chance to share it.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "a == b is True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "a is b is True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "a.qty = 3 raises dataclasses.FrozenInstanceError",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "len({a, b}) == 1",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Adding the field tags: list[str] = [] to Sku works, but every Sku then shares one list",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Python 3.14, no type checker is run:\n\n@dataclass\nclass LogEntry:\n    status: int\n    duration_ms: int\n\nentry = LogEntry(status=\"504\", duration_ms=1830)\nprint(entry.status >= 500)\n\nWhat happens?",
          "explanation": "Type hints are not checked or converted at runtime: the generated __init__ stores whatever it is given, so entry.status is the string \"504\". The bug only shows up later, where the value is used: comparing a str with an int raises TypeError: '>=' not supported between instances of 'str' and 'int'. A type checker such as mypy or pyright would flag the LogEntry(status=\"504\", ...) call before the script runs; at runtime, convert explicitly with int(...) where the text comes in.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "LogEntry(...) raises TypeError because \"504\" is not an int",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The dataclass converts \"504\" to 504, so it prints True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The object is created with status \"504\"; the comparison raises TypeError",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "It prints False, because a str is never >= an int",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
